  "source_text": "Original LessonTrace sample, written during the hackathon.\nIn nested loops, break exits only the nearest enclosing loop. The outer loop can continue with its next item. A second break placed in the outer body stops that outer loop. The indentation and the actual reached line determine which loop stops.\nA function returns its result to its caller. Appending a value changes that list. A loop over an empty list executes its body zero times. An outer list may contain empty rows; the inner loop simply has no values for those rows.\nThe example uses groups A and B, each with values 1 and 2. Its break is inside the inner loop, so each group prints only its first value. These are original examples, not a copied lecture or a claim that quiz performance proves mastery.",
  "source_sha256": "119928004f76ebcdcbff77debf9e3ba87eed0247bd41a8f7de144167cfea2304",
  "starter": "def first_in_each(rows):\n    firsts = []\n    for row in rows:\n        for number in row:\n            firsts.append(number)\n            break\n        break\n    return firsts",
  "reference_solution": "def first_in_each(rows):\n    firsts = []\n    for row in rows:\n        for number in row:\n            firsts.append(number)\n            break\n    return firsts",
  "scope": "One real tutor request plus independent review, actual Python runs. Not a recording of the native UI; not a student learning study.",
  "learner_question": "I expected [4, 5] but this returns [4]. I thought break only stops its nearest loop. Give a small hint about which reached break prevents visiting the second row, using the observed trace. Do not write the corrected program.",
